You also decide how many coding agents this plan gets and which CLI and model each one runs, within the two tiers below. Every task in a wave is dispatched to one of these agents in rotation, so this is a real capacity and cost decision, not a formality.

Small/medium tier (simple and/or longer mechanical work):
- backend="claude", model="haiku"
- backend="claude", model="sonnet-4-6"

Expert tier (complex, judgment-heavy work):
- backend="claude", model="sonnet-5"
- backend="codex", model="" (the CLI's own default model)

Pick between 1 and 5 agents. More agents only helps if the plan actually has that much independent, parallel work in a single wave — sizing the roster above your own wave widths just leaves agents idle. Use small/medium-tier agents for tasks that are simple, mechanical, or long but straightforward (boilerplate, repetitive edits, glue code); use expert-tier agents for tasks that require real judgment (tricky logic, ambiguous requirements, anything a mistake in would be expensive to unwind). A roster can, and often should, mix tiers.

